  "content": {
    "id": "p42-q31-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "31",
    "prompt": "The equation $x^2+px+q=0$ has roots $\\alpha$ and $2\\alpha$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p42-q31-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{p}{3}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\alpha=-\\dfrac{p}{3}$.\n\nUse $\\alpha+2\\alpha=-p$.",
          "worked": "By the sum of roots,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\alpha+2\\alpha&=-p\\\\3\\alpha&=-p\\\\\\alpha&=-\\frac{p}{3}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p42-q31-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $2p^2=9q$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2p^2=9q$.\n\nSubstitute the root $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{p}{3}$ into $x^2+px+q=0$.",
          "worked": "Since $\\alpha$ is a root, $\\alpha^2+p\\alpha+q=0$. Substitute the result from part (a):\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(-\\frac{p}{3}\\right)^2+p\\left(-\\frac{p}{3}\\right)+q&=0\\\\\\frac{p^2}{9}-\\frac{p^2}{3}+q&=0\\\\-2p^2+9q&=0\\\\2p^2&=9q\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
  },
